summary is: {{ A 53 year old male with no significant past medical history, who has developed a swelling in the left elbow over the past week. He noticed it when he was in the shower and it is not painful. It is a firm swelling, it is slightly warm, he is unable to feel fluid inside. It does not affect his range of movement. He is concerned about it and wants to know what is going on. He is not aware of any trauma to the area. He works at a desk and is not physically active. He is fit and well, does not have any other medical problems. He has had osteoarthritis in the past. He is not on any regular medications. He is not allergic to any medication. There is no history of rheumatological illness in the family. He is generally fit and well and does not have any other symptoms.
On examination there is a firm swelling on the underside of the left elbow. There is no tenderness to palpation on the nerve and the surrounding skin is dry. There is no erythema. There is no restriction in movement. There is no sign of infection. There is no redness or tenderness over the tip of the elbow. He is generally fit and well, not pale or anaemic looking. He has dry skin but no other signs of systemic illness. He is not in acute distress.
Diagnosis: Bursitis
Plan: To start oral anti-inflammatory medication, arrange blood tests for inflammatory markers, infection, rheumatological antibodies. Follow up in one week to review blood results and examine the elbow.
Advice: Take Ibuprofen 400mg 2 x a day, take after food, stop if you get heartburn.
Worries: If you notice redness, tenderness or pain in the elbow and it is getting worse please contact us. }}<|eot_id|>
